  "vulnerability_information": "## Summary\n\nA `Set-Cookie` header using HTAB (horizontal tab) instead of SPACE before the `Secure` attribute — e.g. `Set-Cookie: sid=SECRET;\\tSecure` — causes curl to store the cookie without its Secure flag. The cookie is then sent over plaintext HTTP on subsequent requests to the same host.\n\nThe space-separated form `Set-Cookie: sid=SECRET; Secure` works correctly.\n\n## Affected versions\n\ncurl 8.13.0 through at least 8.21.0 (all versions containing commit `1aea05a6c26`, \"cookie: convert to using strparse\", 2025-02-18).\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `lib/cookie.c`, `parse_cookie_header()` uses a do/while loop that parses each cookie attribute:\n\n```c\ndo {\n    if(!curlx_str_cspn(&ptr, &name, \";\\t\\r\\n=\")) {\n        // parse attribute\n    }\n} while(!curlx_str_single(&ptr, ';'));\n```\n\nAfter consuming the `;` separator, `ptr` points at the HTAB. Since `\\t` is in the reject set of `curlx_str_cspn`, the function sees it as the first character, returns an empty match (failure), and the `if` block is skipped. Then `curlx_str_single(&ptr, ';')` fails because the current byte is `\\t`, not `;`, so the loop exits. The `Secure` attribute is never parsed, but the cookie is still stored.\n\nThe previous parser (before `1aea05a6c26`) had `while(ISBLANK(*ptr)) ptr++;` before parsing each attribute, which correctly skipped HTAB. This blank-skipping was lost in the strparse conversion. The helper `curlx_str_passblanks()` exists in the codebase but is not called here.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n### Expected vs actual output\n\n**Expected:** Neither cookie is sent over HTTP (both have `Secure`).\n\n**Actual:**\n\n```\nHTAB  HTTP follow-up: sid=HTAB_SECRET      <-- leaked over HTTP\nSPACE HTTP follow-up: <none>               <-- correctly withheld\nHTAB  jar secure flag: FALSE               <-- wrong\nSPACE jar secure flag: TRUE                <-- correct\n```\n\n## Impact\n\nThe `Secure` flag is a confidentiality boundary that prevents cookies from being sent over unencrypted connections. Silently dropping it means a session cookie intended to be HTTPS-only is exposed on any subsequent HTTP request to the same host. A network attacker observing that HTTP traffic can capture the cookie. All attributes at or after the HTAB position are similarly skipped (e.g. `HttpOnly`).\n\nHTAB is valid optional whitespace per both RFC 6265 section 5.2 and the HTTP grammar (OWS). The old parser handled it correctly.",
